Package: fmake
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, python3 (>= 3.11)
Recommends: pkgconf | pkg-config, binutils
Suggests: ninja-build, make
Description: build tool for C and C++ that needs no build file
 fmake works out what to build by reading the source. A translation unit
 defining main() at file scope is a program; the link set is the transitive
 closure over undefined symbols, taken from object symbol tables rather than
 from the #include graph, so it is exact rather than heuristic. Libraries are
 chosen the same way: an undefined SDL_Init proves SDL is called, where
 including SDL.h only proves a declaration was wanted.
 .
 What cannot be inferred is written down next to the code it describes, in
 Doxygen-format comments, so the same comment documents and builds. A small
 Makefile-syntax file carries rules, and a TOML file the handful of facts that
 belong to no single file.
 .
 Qt is handled without a build file: moc, uic and rcc are driven by Q_OBJECT,
 by the ui_*.h a source includes, and by the resource paths it opens.
 .
 fmake --eject writes a standalone Makefile or build.ninja and gets out of the
 way, producing binaries that match its own symbol for symbol.
